  "query_preview": "..\n\nPersistence forecast as ridging aloft with surface high pressure \nmaintains influence over the Great Lakes. Expecting the blocking \npattern to hold through this weekend. Models pointing toward this \nmid-upper level pattern breaking down into zonal flow early-mid \nweek, but surface high pressure will continue to influence the \nregional weather.  \n\nFog will again be the main concern tonight as it has been the last \nfew nights within this pattern. Flow remains easterly tonight with \nLake Erie supplying the lower level moisture with clear skies \nproviding the favorable radiational cooling for fog development. \nThere is some indication on some model soundings that there is a \nlittle more dry air at the surface compared to last night, at least \nfor eastern portions of the CWA. However, patchy fog will be \npossible across most of the forecast area tonight. Fog potential \nwill be in the forecast for each night and the following morning \nthrough the weekend with the typical lifting of fog by around mid \nor late morning. \n\nThermal profiles moderate a little bit through the weekend. \nTemperatures generally around the mid 60s today and tomorrow will \nclimb to around 70 degrees for Sunday. The low that flattens the \nridge aloft will send a backdoor cold front through the region early \nnext week and bring cooler temperatures. The front will have very \nlittle moisture to work with, so expecting a dry frontal passage. \nTemperatures at 850mb drop into the single digit C range during the \nearly week. Tuesday offering mid 50s to low 60s high for Tuesday. \nGulf/southeast US ridge directs any precipitations chances to our \nwest and north during the mid-week period as well.",
